2026-09-14","paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"External debt accelerating: \u00a3174m increase in one year, projected 42% growth over five years","lead":"Rapid debt accumulation increases fixed interest obligations and reduces future spending flexibility, particularly if council revenue growth slows relative to interest costs.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Internal borrowing reserves collapsed in one year, forcing shift to external debt markets","lead":"Depletion of internal cash reserves reduces financial flexibility and forces the council into external debt markets for all new capital spending, increasing vulnerability to market disruption.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Council","paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","headline":"Quarter of debt-funded service investments are loaned to subsidiaries, with i4b Holdings loans growing sharply","detail":"Of \u00a3287.5m in service investments funded by borrowing, i4b Holdings receives \u00a3179.2m (projected to grow to \u00a3211.2m by 2026/27) and First Wave Housing receives \u00a333.4m. Brent is borrowing externally to fund loans and equity in subsidiary companies.","category":"capital","matters":"A concentrated portion of the council's external borrowing funds subsidiary operations rather than direct council services, creating risk if those subsidiaries underperform.","figures":"Service investments funded by borrowing \u00a3287.5m (2025/26); i4b Holdings loans \u00a3179.2m growing to \u00a3211.2m; i4b Holdings equity \u00a343.9m to \u00a351.9m","quote":"i4b Holdings Ltd Subsidiary Loans* 182.4 500.0 179.2 211.2 211.2 211.2","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Council","paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","headline":"External debt accelerating: \u00a3174m increase in one year, projected 42% growth over five years","detail":"External loans grew from \u00a3900.0m to \u00a31,074.1m (19.3% in 2025/26 alone) and are forecast to reach \u00a31,274.6m by 2028/29. Gross interest payable rose from \u00a341.1m to projected \u00a353.4m by 2027/28.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Rapid debt accumulation increases fixed interest obligations and reduces future spending flexibility, particularly if council revenue growth slows relative to interest costs.","figures":"External Loans 2024/25 \u00a3900.0m, 2025/26 \u00a31,074.1m, 2028/29 \u00a31,274.6m; Interest Payable \u00a341.1m to \u00a353.4m projected","quote":"External Loans 900.0 1,074.1 1,174.0 1,235.2 1,274.6","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Council","paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","headline":"Internal borrowing reserves collapsed in one year, forcing shift to external debt markets","detail":"Internal borrowing fell from \u00a3383.2m to \u00a3276.5m (down \u00a3106.7m) in a single year, while CFR grew from \u00a31,353.8m to \u00a31,412.3m. The council's own financial reserves are being exhausted, forcing reliance on external borrowing to fund future capital needs.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Depletion of internal cash reserves reduces financial flexibility and forces the council into external debt markets for all new capital spending, increasing vulnerability to market disruption.","figures":"Internal Borrowing 2024/25 \u00a3383.2m down to 2025/26 \u00a3276.5m; CFR increased from \u00a31,353.8m to \u00a31,412.3m","quote":"Internal Borrowing 383.2 276.5 336.5 324.0 301.2","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Council","paper_title":"12. Vice Chairs Report from the Audit and Standards Advisory Committee - September 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159651/159651.pdf","headline":"The council is responding to a C3 regulatory judgement from the Regulator of Social Housing","detail":"The Audit and Standards Advisory Committee received an update in February 2026 on the Council's response to findings from the Regulator of Social Housing following a C3 regulatory judgement. The committee noted improvement activity being undertaken in response. The paper does not specify what the C3 judgement found or what remedial actions are required.","category":"governance","matters":"A C3 regulatory judgement from the housing regulator indicates significant regulatory concerns about governance or service delivery. A lender would want to assess implications for creditworthiness, and a finance director needs to track remediation progress.","figures":"","quote":"The report provided an update on the Council's response to the findings of the Regulator of Social Housing and the improvement activity being undertaken following the Council's C3 regulatory judgement.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Council","paper_title":"13. Treasury Management Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159652/159652.pdf","headline":"Gross borrowing increased \u00a3174m in one year to \u00a31,074m to fund capital programme, representing 19% leverage increase","detail":"Total external borrowing rose from \u00a3900.0m to \u00a31,074.1m. The council raised \u00a3295.0m of new loans (\u00a3230m from PWLB at average rates rising to 5.7% for 10-year maturity, \u00a365m from local authorities at 4.4%) and repaid \u00a3121.0m of existing debt. Combined with declining internal borrowing, this shows the council is now reliant on external markets for capital programme funding.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Rapid leverage increase without corresponding cash generation raises questions about sustainability of capital programme funding and exposure to further rate rises","figures":"Borrowing increased \u00a3174.1m; new PWLB borrowing at 5.7% (10-year); internal borrowing down \u00a3115.6m; net borrowing rose to \u00a31,030.3m","quote":"External borrowing increased from \u00a3900.0m to \u00a31,074.1m during the year, reflecting the raising of \u00a3295.0m of new loans and the repayment of \u00a3121.0m of existing debt.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-14"},"name":"Brent","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Quarter of debt-funded service investments are loaned to subsidiaries, with i4b Holdings loans growing sharply","lead":"A concentrated portion of the council's external borrowing funds subsidiary operations rather than direct council services, creating risk if those subsidiaries underperform.","also":["15 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Standards Advisory Committee on 2026-09-23","n_findings":16,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"13d. Appendix 4- Prudential Indicators 2025-26 Outturn","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.brent.gov.uk/documents/s159666/159666.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":172,"news_drafts_total":3}}